## Important Experimental Rules

For fair LLM backend comparison:

1. Keep the three expert extractors unchanged.
2. Keep the threshold files unchanged.
3. Keep the candidate registry logic unchanged unless there is a true bug.
4. Keep source inputs identical.
5. Keep decoding settings as consistent as possible, especially `--llm_temperature 0`.
6. Save each backend into a separate output folder.
7. Compare the same aligned records across backends.
8. Do not hard-code Hugging Face tokens.

Recommended output layout:

```text
Outputs2/qwen/
Outputs2/llama31_8b/
Outputs2/mistral7b/
```

For policy experiments, use explicit folder names:

```text
Outputs2/qwen_strict/
Outputs2/qwen_pubmedbert_plus_verified/
```


## Understanding The Metrics

### Exact span metrics

Exact span metrics require label, start offset, and end offset to match the gold annotation exactly. These are very strict. A span with the right meaning but one extra word will be counted as false positive and false negative.

### Relaxed span metrics

Relaxed span metrics use character intersection-over-union. They are useful when boundaries are close but not exact.

### Character-level metrics

Character-level metrics show whether predicted spans overlap the right gold text, even when exact boundaries differ.

### Token-level metrics

Token-level metrics are usually the most important for the current experiments. They evaluate final selected spans at the token level and report:

```text
precision
recall
F1
accuracy
```

Overall token assignment accuracy is different from one-vs-rest accuracy. One-vs-rest accuracy can look very high because most tokens are negative for each label. Token F1 is usually more informative for extraction quality.

### Registry oracle recall

Registry oracle recall asks: if the selection layer were perfect, how much gold could be recovered from all candidates in the registry?

If registry oracle recall is much higher than final F1, the problem is selection/filtering, not candidate generation.

### Selection ablations

Use selection ablations to understand where errors come from:

```text
pubmedbert_only
majority_vote
pubmedbert_anchor
pubmedbert_plus_verified
registry_filtered
registry_all
```

If `pubmedbert_only` beats `final`, the multi-agent layer is hurting precision or selection quality. If `registry_all` has much higher recall but low precision, the registry contains useful candidates but needs better filtering.


## Current Interpretation From Recent Runs

Recent results showed this pattern:

```text
PubMedBERT-only: strongest current production baseline
Final strict multi-agent: slightly higher recall but much lower precision
Majority vote: higher recall but too many false positives
Registry all: useful oracle signal but poor filtering
```

Therefore, the current best next experiment is:

```text
pubmedbert_plus_verified
```

This makes PubMedBERT the anchor and uses the LLM agents as conservative verification/rescue, not as the primary selector.


## Common Problems And Fixes

### Connection error

Usually vLLM is not running, is running on a different port, or the Python script is pointing at the wrong base URL.

Check:

```bash
curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/models
```

Then make sure `--llm_backend` matches the served model.

### Model 404

The served model name does not match the requested model name. Use:

```bash
Labelcentered/scripts/start_vllm_backend.sh <backend>
```

or pass the exact served model name with `--llm_model`.

### Permission denied when running start script

Run it through bash:

```bash
bash Labelcentered/scripts/start_vllm_backend.sh qwen
```

or make it executable:

```bash
chmod +x Labelcentered/scripts/start_vllm_backend.sh
```

### Request timed out

The model may be slow or the prompt may be too large. Try:

```text
--doctor_batch_size 3
--llm_max_tokens 2048
--llm_timeout_seconds 1800
--llm_temperature 0
--skip_existing
```

### Invalid LLM structured output

This is more common with Mistral. The client already attempts JSON repair and an unstructured fallback, but some records may still fail. Smaller doctor batches and more output tokens can help.

### Gold annotation text/source mismatches

The evaluator now attempts offset/text repair for Label Studio annotations when whitespace or escaped newlines cause mismatches. The report distinguishes:

```text
Gold annotation text/source mismatches
Gold annotation offset/text repairs
```

A mismatch count of 0 with repairs > 0 means evaluation alignment was repaired successfully.


## Recommended Smoke Test Before A Full Run

Before running all 10 records, run one record:

```bash
python Labelcentered/run_json_labelcentered.py \
  --json ./Labelcentered/Ex_171_180_Mar.3_2026.json \
  --llm_backend qwen \
  --out_dir ./Outputs2/qwen_test1 \
  --limit_records 1 \
  --doctor_batch_size 10 \
  --max_rounds 3 \
  --llm_max_tokens 2048 \
  --llm_temperature 0
```

Then evaluate:

```bash
python Labelcentered/evaluate_labelcentered.py \
  --gold ./Labelcentered/Ex_171_180_Mar.3_2026.json \
  --pred_dir ./Outputs2/qwen_test1 \
  --out_json ./Outputs2/qwen_test1/evaluation.json \
  --out_txt ./Outputs2/qwen_test1/evaluation.txt
```

If the output validates and evaluation aligns one record, then run all 10 records.


## Development Checks

Run the test suite from inside the Labelcentered folder:

```bash
PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX=/private/tmp/labelcentered_pycache python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v
```

The test suite covers backend settings, candidate registry, expansion, consensus, doctor completeness, LLM JSON parsing, output validation, RAG guideline retrieval, evaluation, and the selection policy.


## Practical Summary

The project compares LLM agent backends for a fixed extraction pipeline. The three encoder expert models create candidate spans. The LLM agents review and adjudicate candidates, but final spans must always come from the validated registry. Evaluation focuses on final selected spans, especially token-level precision, recall, F1, and accuracy.

For current experiments, use `expert_consensus_verified` as the default equal-expert production policy, `strict` as the original unanimous-consensus baseline, and `pubmedbert_plus_verified` only as a historical PubMedBERT-first ablation. Re-evaluate existing outputs to inspect ablations, and rerun extraction only when you need the selected final output itself to use a different final selection policy.
